<%!-- A draft, written from what the code actually does. It describes the three
      third parties that run on the site, the two cookies Ethos sets itself,
      and how to refuse the first group. It makes no claim about any
      jurisdiction, and the contact line names no address because none has been
      supplied — see the task report. --%>
<div class="mx-auto max-w-2xl px-4 py-10">
  <header>
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold">Privacy</h1>
    <p class="mt-1 text-ink-muted">
      What Ethos collects, which third parties run on the site, and how to refuse them.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="mt-8">
    <h2 class="font-semibold">Third parties that run on this site</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink">
      Up to three companies run code in your browser. Each one is here for a different
      reason, and each one can see something different. The first two run on every page of
      this site; the third runs on some. The one exception is the embeddable foliage map
      at <code>/foliage/embed</code>, which other sites place in a frame of their own: it
      loads none of the three.
    </p>

    <h3 class="mt-6 font-semibold">PostHog — product analytics</h3>
    <p class="mt-2 text-ink">
      PostHog records which pages are viewed and which links are clicked, along with the
      browser and device making the request and an approximate location derived from the IP
      address. So that it can tell one visit from another, it stores an identifier on your
      device — in your browser's local storage and in a first-party cookie of its own. It is what
      tells us which guides people actually read. Where consent is required, PostHog loads
      only after it is given, and not before.
    </p>

    <h3 class="mt-6 font-semibold">Google AdSense — advertising, and the consent banner</h3>
    <p class="mt-2 text-ink">
      Ads on this site are served by Google AdSense, which sets its own cookies and uses
      them to choose and measure ads. The consent banner is also Google's: it is a
      Google-certified consent management platform, and the choice you make in it is the
      signal the rest of this page refers to.
    </p>

    <h3 class="mt-6 font-semibold">GetYourGuide — affiliate links</h3>
    <p class="mt-2 text-ink">
      Some pages carry a GetYourGuide widget listing tours and activities: guides, place
      pages, collections and destination hubs about a place GetYourGuide covers. If
      you book through one, Ethos earns a commission at no extra cost to you, and
      GetYourGuide sets cookies to attribute the booking. Where consent is required, the
      widget loads only after it is given.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-10">
    <h2 class="font-semibold">Cookies Ethos sets itself</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink">
      Two, set by the site itself rather than by any of the third parties above, and
      neither used for advertising.
    </p>
    <ul class="mt-3 space-y-2 text-ink">
      <li>
        A <strong>session</strong>
        cookie, which carries the flash messages you see after an action and, if you are
        signed in, the token identifying you. It is cleared when you log out.
      </li>
      <li>
        A <strong>remember</strong>
        me cookie, valid for 60 days, which keeps you signed in between visits. It is set
        only if you ask for it while logging in, and only for accounts. A reader who never
        signs in never receives one.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-10">
    <h2 class="font-semibold">What the site records on its own servers</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink">
      Each guide keeps a count of how many times it has been read. It is a single number on
      the guide, not a record of who read it.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink">
      If you make an account, Ethos stores the email address and username you give it and
      your password, hashed — the password itself is never stored and cannot be read back.
      It also stores what you do with the account: the guides you write, the comments and
      suggestions you leave, and the thumbs up and thumbs down you give, each recorded
      against your account. It also records any badges you have earned — for each, the
      badge's name and the date it was awarded. Deleting an account or its contents is a
      request to the site owner.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-10">
    <h2 class="font-semibold">Changing your mind</h2>
    <%!-- "Can be reopened" is a claim about the CMP, not about this repo. Nothing here
          provides a reopen control and no later task adds one: the revocation entry point
          is configured in Google's Funding Choices dashboard, and Google's TCF policy
          requires withdrawal to be possible, so the sentence is true of a correctly
          configured CMP. Task 5 verifies it against the live banner. Do NOT delete this
          sentence for looking unverifiable — without it the page tells readers nothing
          about how to change their mind. If Task 5 finds no reopen control, fix the CMP
          configuration, then this line. --%>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink">
      The consent banner can be reopened, and the choice you make in it replaces the one
      before. Refusing after having accepted stops the scripts loading on your next page
      view; it does not reach back and delete what was already collected.
    </p>
    <%!-- The US control is a different mechanism from the banner above, not a
          restatement of it. The EEA regime is opt-in, so nothing loads until the
          banner is answered; the US state regimes are opt-out, so analytics and the
          affiliate widget run until this link is used. Both are served by the same
          Google CMP, and `assets/js/consent.js` reads both signals — the TCF one for
          the banner, the Global Privacy Platform one for this link. --%>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink">
      In US states with a privacy law that provides one, a
      <strong>Do Not Sell or Share My Personal Information</strong>
      link appears at the
      foot of the page. Using it records an opt-out, and PostHog and the GetYourGuide
      widget stop loading from your next page view. If your browser sends a Global
      Privacy Control signal, that is honoured the same way and you need not use the
      link at all.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink">
      Every cookie described here, first-party and third-party alike, can be cleared in
      your browser's settings, and your browser can be told to refuse them.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-10">
    <h2 class="font-semibold">Contact</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink">
      For a question about any of this, or to ask what is held about you, contact the site
      owner.
    </p>
  </section>

  <p class="mt-10 text-sm text-ink-muted">Last updated 6 September 2026.</p>
</div>
